WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.700
I can't advertise my work as Native American
art, which it is.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.140
This law is anti-competitive and the purpose
of it and the effect of it

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.420
is to discriminate against Native American
art from other states.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.360
It flips on it's head the whole concept of
truth in advertising.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.619
Peggy Fontenot began her Native art career
decades ago

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.200
working in beading, silver jewelry and photography.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.840
When I started photographing, I thought that
people ruined my photographs

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:36.040
and then I realized that the people were the
stories and that they made the photograph.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.560
She's a part of the Patawomeck tribe

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.019
and has built a career that has taken her
everywhere.

00:00:40.019 --> 00:00:42.800
I travel all over the country selling my work.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.389
I show at different museum shows.

00:00:46.389 --> 00:00:53.480
For 4 years I did the jewelry for the Kathy
Ireland Sports Illustrated swimsuit calendars.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.040
I felt it was a real honor to be included.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.100
Everything was going great with her career,

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:02.799
that was until 2016 when a new law passed
in Oklahoma and changed her entire world.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.720
Members this is House bill 2261,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.860
it provides safeguards for Native American artists

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.560
and for the collectors of Native Indian art.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.420
That's state representative Chuck Hoskin,
the writer of the bill.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.409
The new law made it so only people from federally
recognized tribes

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:19.240
can call themselves Native artists.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.440
Peggy belongs to a state recognized tribe

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.740
and so, thanks to this new Oklahoma law,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.100
she can no longer market her art as Native
American in Oklahoma.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.840
That's Fontenot's attorney Anastasia Boden
with the Pacific Legal Foundation.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.009
She's representing Fontenot in a lawsuit against
the state

00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:36.890
saying the new law violates Fontenot's free
speech rights.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:43.340
To call every state recognized tribe fake
and illegitimate

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.760
is just broad sweeping and wrong.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.860
She could also face fines or imprisonment
if she describes her work as she always has.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:52.140
I can still sell my work in Oklahoma

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.220
but I can't advertise it as Native American
made, which it is.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.219
That's actually the whole point of the law

00:01:58.219 --> 00:02:00.609
is to narrow the universe of people

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:05.119
who can describe particular art as American
Indian art.

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:08.580
Clark Neily is the vice president of the Libertarian
think tank the Cato Institute,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.740
and spent 17 years as an attorney at the public
interest law firm The Institute for Justice.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:17.390
Very clearly some group of people want to
prevent other people from using that terminology

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.860
presumably because it has some value in the
marketplace.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.030
Oklahoma is central to Native American artists.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.360
They put on some of the biggest Native American
art shows in the country.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.620
Each year Peggy goes to the Red Earth Festival,

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:37.460
where thousands of people come to either show
their art or to see Native American art.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.000
Peggy will miss out on earning a living in
Oklahoma,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.680
she makes her money by going to these art
shows.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.940
She's really upset because she thinks that
this is free speech

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:53.400
and she's not able to truthfully call herself
what she is.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.120
It completely undercuts and flips on it's
head the whole concept of truth in advertising.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.560
My bead work and my work isn't going to have
the same importance

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.620
if it's not being marketed as what it is,
which is Native American.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.380
Hoskin declined our interview request but
he said in a statement:

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.200
'we have seen people time and time again falsely
claim heritage

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.760
in order to market themselves as Native artists.'

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.560
and 'this closes a loophole in the federal
act here in Oklahoma

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.730
protecting Oklahoma Indian artists.'

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.610
Rebecca Tushnet is a Harvard Law professor
who studies truth in advertising law.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:31.240
She says it may have been poorly crafted,
but the Oklahoma law was well intentioned.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:36.330
I think they were interested in the same basic
question as the Federal Government was

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:41.260
which is 'how can people sell this stuff and
how can they be truthful about what it is?'

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.730
Tushnet says that sellers misrepresenting
their products

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.980
is a significant problem in the Native art
world

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.880
and that a federal law that defines a Native
artist more broadly has done some good.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:54.840
The reason, at least the federal law was enacted
was a fair amount of fraud in the market

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.980
which was doing harm to legitimate artisans.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.960
For example, five people were indicted in
July 2017

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.400
after being accused of importing and selling
fake Native American jewelry from the Philippines.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.380
The five suspects are accused of violating
the Indian Arts and Crafts Act.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.720
But Boden says the purpose of the Oklahoma
law is to restrict competition.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.180
This law is anti competitive and the purpose
of it and the effect of it

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.760
is to discriminate against Native American
art from other states.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.520
They want to sell their art

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:25.720
and if they can limit the number of people
who can compete with them in that space

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.100
then presumably you limit competition you
expect prices to go up, it's simple economics.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.260
Federally recognized tribes are a big constituency
in Oklahoma.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.160
And Hoskin isn't just the author of the bill,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.920
he's also served as the chief of staff for
the Cherokee Nation.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.820
And of course the Cherokees are a federally
recognized tribe

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.490
and certainly would have an interest in putting
a law on the books

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:49.350
that says that only federally recognized tribes
can call themselves Native American.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.130
The five big federally recognized tribes

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:54.280
did officially come out in support of the
state against Fontenot.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.960
For me to come out by myself and challenge
these tribes,

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.380
I definitely feel that I'm up against the
world if you will.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:07.870
Businesses or even individuals who are facing
competition

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:10.520
have essentially two ways to come out ahead.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.200
One is to up their own game,

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.580
or you can go to the government and try to
sandbag your competitors.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.600
I've seen this kind of thing over and over
and over again.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.300
Whether it's organic food or interior designers
or catfish.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:25.540
It is now the case that of the 2500 different
species of catfish in the world

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.970
you can only sell in America one particular
fish as catfish

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:30.680
and that's the North American Channel Catfish.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.580
Why? because that's the kind that we raise
here in America

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:35.770
and the people who raise that fish went and
got a law passed

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.260
that says that's the only kind you can call
a catfish.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.400
Fontenot's lawyers were able to stop the enforcement
of the law temporarily

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.880
so that she and artists like her can continue
to show their art in the state.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.139
HB 2261 is still on the books though so it
isn't a permanent solution yet.

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:56.610
If we're not successful and this law stands
and it goes to other states

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.860
at the end of the day, I'm sill Indian.

